{"text": " Mortgage rates just hit 7%. Higher rates are supposed to be bringing prices down. The buyers from 2021 are locked in at rates around 3%. Selling means handing that cheap debt back and taking on expensive debt at today's prices. The agency that regulates Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, the two government-backed companies standing behind $6.6 trillion of American home loans, measured it. They found that for every point today's rate sits above the rate an owner already has, the odds they sell fall by about 18%. Between spring 2022 and the end of 2023, they estimate that wiped out 1.7 million home sales that would otherwise have happened, which caused rising interest rates to have an opposite effect on housing. We saw higher rates decrease prices by a little over 3%, but the results from the supply reduction and the sellers who vanished pushed home prices up by nearly 6%. So by the agency's own measure, the rate lock effect did more than offset the rate increase. The tool built to cool prices took the sellers out of the market instead and caused prices to rise.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.02, "text": " Mortgage rates just hit 7%.", "tokens": [50363, 45446, 3965, 655, 2277, 767, 7225, 50469], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.1396638430081881, "compression_ratio": 1.590625, "no_speech_prob": 0.0030819005332887173, "words": [{"word": " Mortgage", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.32, "probability": 0.2921053171157837}, {"word": " rates", "start": 0.32, "end": 0.64, "probability": 0.9853444695472717}, {"word": " just", "start": 0.64, "end": 0.88, "probability": 0.9609113335609436}, {"word": " hit", "start": 0.88, "end": 1.08, "probability": 0.9565796852111816}, {"word": " 7%.", "start": 1.08, "end": 2.02, "probability": 0.6759105622768402}]}, {"id": 1, "seek": 0, "start": 2.02, "end": 4.56, "text": " Higher rates are supposed to be bringing prices down.", "tokens": [50469, 16038, 3965, 389, 4385, 284, 307, 6079, 4536, 866, 13, 50601], "temper